import{_ as t,o,c as s,ag as a}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"Git hooks and workflows","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"components/githooks.md","filePath":"components/githooks.md"}'),i={name:"components/githooks.md"};function n(d,e,r,h,c,l){return o(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<h1 id="git-hooks-and-workflows" tabindex="-1">Git hooks and workflows <a class="header-anchor" href="#git-hooks-and-workflows" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Git hooks and workflows&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A git hook is a program that git runs at a fixed point, for example before a commit or before a push. When the hook exits with a status other than 0, git refuses the commit or the push. A GitHub Actions workflow is a file that tells GitHub which jobs to run on a push or a pull request.</p><p>The files of this page are in <code>githooks/</code> and <code>githooks/workflows/</code>. Most of them are templates for the Julia repositories of the research tree; the others are scripts beside them. Two subcommands of the <code>harness</code> command install the templates into every repository below <code>Packages/</code> and <code>Experiments/</code> (see <a href="./../research-tree#clone-sync-and-trust">Clone, sync and trust</a>):</p><ul><li><code>harness githooks --apply</code> copies <code>pre-commit</code>, <code>pre-commit.jl</code>, <code>pre-push</code>, <code>pre-push.jl</code> and <code>scripts/test-layout.jl</code> into <code>.githooks/</code> of each repository, and sets <code>core.hooksPath</code> there.</li><li><code>harness workflows --apply</code> copies the files of <code>githooks/workflows/</code> into each repository that has a <code>Project.toml</code>.</li></ul><p><code>harness wiki-lint-hook --apply</code> installs <code>pre-commit-wiki</code> into the prose repositories. Each copy is byte-identical to its template and is a tracked file in the repository. Edit the template, then install again; never edit a copy. The other scripts of this page run from the checkout. This repository has a pre-commit and a pre-push hook of its own, <code>.githooks/pre-commit</code> and <code>.githooks/pre-push</code>; they are not templates, and <a href="./../development">Development</a> describes them.</p><h2 id="pre-commit-pre-commit-jl-and-pre-commit-test-jl" tabindex="-1"><code>pre-commit</code>, <code>pre-commit.jl</code> and <code>pre-commit-test.jl</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#pre-commit-pre-commit-jl-and-pre-commit-test-jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`pre-commit`, `pre-commit.jl` and `pre-commit-test.jl`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>pre-commit</code> is the shared pre-commit hook of the Julia repositories. It is a short bash launcher that starts <code>pre-commit.jl</code> beside it, in one Julia process. A commit with no staged <code>.jl</code> path, a deletion included, and no change below <code>test/</code> passes without starting <code>julia</code>. Without <code>julia</code> on the <code>PATH</code>, any other commit is refused with <code>pre-commit: julia is not on the PATH.</code> The program runs these stages:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">stage</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it checks</th><th style="text-align:left;">on a failure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">0</td><td style="text-align:left;">the <code>violations</code> of <code>test-layout.jl</code>, on a commit that touches <code>test/</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">blocks</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">1</td><td style="text-align:left;">JuliaFormatter 2.14 or later, with the <code>.JuliaFormatter.toml</code> of the repository</td><td style="text-align:left;">blocks</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">2</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>fatou lint</code>, with a limit of 60 s</td><td style="text-align:left;">warns only</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">2.5</td><td style="text-align:left;">each file is in Unicode NFC</td><td style="text-align:left;">blocks</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">3</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>using &lt;package&gt;</code> loads, with a limit of 600 s, for a repository with a package</td><td style="text-align:left;">blocks</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Stages 1 to 2.5 run on the staged <code>.jl</code> files only, a renamed file under its new name and a file that became a symlink too; a path with non-ASCII characters is read as it is, not in git&#39;s quoted form. A staged <code>.jl</code> path that is not a file, such as a symlink to a missing file or to a directory, is refused with one line that names it, before any stage runs. Stage 3 runs when a <code>.jl</code> file is staged, and also when only the deletion of one is: a deletion can break an <code>include</code>. Stage 1 checks each file on its own: a file that does not parse fails stage 1, with the first two lines of its error on stderr, the message and its location, and the other files are still checked. Stage 1 loads JuliaFormatter from the harness&#39;s Julia environment, which pins 2.14, and refuses an older release with one line that names its version, marking every staged file; JuliaFormatter 2.14 supports Julia 1.10 and later. Stage 1 has no time limit (K130 of <code>KNOWN_ISSUES.md</code>). Stage 0 reads <code>.githooks/test-layout.jl</code>, which <code>harness githooks</code> installs beside the hook; a missing copy blocks the commit. Every stage reads the working tree, not the index (K128). The lint only warns, because <code>fatou lint</code> reports false positives; without <code>fatou</code> on the <code>PATH</code>, or with a <code>fatou</code> that cannot start, the stage prints one line and is skipped. The staged paths reach <code>fatou</code> after <code>--</code>. A finding is a line of the form <code>&lt;path&gt;:&lt;line&gt;:&lt;col&gt;: …</code>; a non-zero exit of <code>fatou</code> with no such line is a failure of <code>fatou</code>, which the stage reports with its exit status and skips. The NFC stage does not change a file; run <code>nfc.jl --apply</code> to fix one. Stage 3 loads the package in a child process, in the environment of the repository, so that the versions of JuliaFormatter&#39;s dependencies in the hook&#39;s own process do not change what the package loads. A failed load shows at most 20 lines of its output, from the first line that holds <code>ERROR:</code>, or from its first line when no line does. A repository has a package when its <code>Project.toml</code>, read as TOML, has a <code>name</code> and a <code>uuid</code>. A <code>Project.toml</code> that does not parse refuses the commit with one line that holds the first two lines of the error, the message and its location. A package name that <code>Base.isidentifier</code> refuses, or that the parser of the running Julia does not read as that one name, such as the keyword <code>end</code>, refuses the commit with one line, and no load test runs. The lint stage and the load test run in a process group of their own: when the limit runs out, the hook sends the group SIGTERM, and SIGKILL 5 s later, and does not wait for the output of a process that left the group. When you interrupt the hook, it kills that group on its way out. A commit with no staged <code>.jl</code> path and no change below <code>test/</code> passes with no check. <code>git commit --no-verify</code> skips the hook.</p><p><code>pre-commit-test.jl</code> runs both hooks, unchanged, in fixture package repositories (<code>julia --startup-file=no pre-commit-test.jl</code>). For <code>pre-commit</code>: a formatted file, also under a non-ASCII name, passes, and a misformatted file, a renamed misformatted file, a file that does not parse, a file that does not load, a file that is not NFC and a change below <code>test/</code> without <code>test-layout.jl</code> or out of its layout are refused, and so is a JuliaFormatter older than 2.14; a file that does not parse beside a formatted one is listed alone; a staged symlink to a missing file or to a directory is refused with one line; a <code>Project.toml</code> with an indented, single-quoted name runs the load test, one with a <code>name</code> and no <code>uuid</code> runs none, one that does not parse is refused with the error&#39;s location, and so is one whose name is not an identifier, without running the code that the name holds, or is the keyword <code>end</code>; a file that does not parse is listed with the error&#39;s location; a failed load report starts at its <code>ERROR:</code> line; a commit that only deletes a <code>.jl</code> file runs the load test; without <code>julia</code>, or with a <code>julia</code> that cannot start, a commit with no <code>.jl</code> file and no <code>test/</code> path passes; without <code>julia</code>, any other commit, a <code>.jl</code> file that became a symlink too, is refused; a missing <code>fatou</code>, a <code>fatou</code> that cannot start, a <code>fatou</code> that fails, and the findings of <code>fatou</code>, give their lines, and the staged paths reach <code>fatou</code> after <code>--</code>; a commit of one <code>.jl</code> file starts <code>julia</code> from the <code>PATH</code> once; an interrupted commit stops its load test. It checks the time limits of the lint stage and the load test, also for a child that holds the output and for a process that leaves the group, that the time box sends no SIGKILL after it returns, and that <code>pre-commit.jl</code> and <code>pre-push.jl</code> are in SciML style. For <code>pre-push</code> it checks each case of the gate and the suite. CI runs it on Linux.</p><h2 id="pre-push-and-pre-push-jl" tabindex="-1"><code>pre-push</code> and <code>pre-push.jl</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#pre-push-and-pre-push-jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`pre-push` and `pre-push.jl`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>pre-push</code> is the shared pre-push hook of the Julia repositories. Like <code>pre-commit</code>, it is a bash launcher that starts <code>pre-push.jl</code> beside it, and passes it git&#39;s ref lines on stdin. A push that updates no <code>main</code> or <code>master</code> passes without starting <code>julia</code>. Without <code>julia</code> on the <code>PATH</code>, a push of either is refused with <code>pre-push: julia is not on the PATH.</code> The program runs the full test suite, but only on a push to <code>main</code> or <code>master</code>. A push to another branch passes at once, and CI tests it.</p><p>For a package, the hook runs <code>Pkg.test(&quot;&lt;package&gt;&quot;)</code> with <code>--check-bounds=auto</code>, and passes the flag to the test process through <code>julia_args</code>: up to Julia 1.12, <code>Pkg.test</code> gives the test process <code>--check-bounds=yes</code> whatever the calling process uses. A repository has a package when its <code>Project.toml</code>, read as TOML, has a <code>name</code> and a <code>uuid</code>. For a repository with no package, it runs <code>test/runtests.jl</code> in the root environment, which ignores <code>test/Project.toml</code> (K134). With neither, it runs nothing and passes. A <code>Project.toml</code> that does not parse refuses the push with one line that holds the first two lines of the error, the message and its location. A package name that <code>Base.isidentifier</code> refuses, or that the parser of the running Julia does not read as that one name, such as a keyword, refuses the push with one line before any suite starts. A failure of the suite refuses the push with <code>ERROR — tests must pass before pushing to main or master.</code> The suite runs in the working tree, not on the pushed commit (K133).</p><p>The suite takes 10 to 30 minutes and prints nothing until it ends. That is not a hang. The suite runs in a process group of its own. When you interrupt the hook with Ctrl-C or <code>SIGINT</code>, it sends the interrupt to that group, waits until no process of the group is left, and exits with status 130. When a process of the group still runs 30 s after the interrupt, also after the suite itself has ended, the hook sends SIGKILL to the group. A second interrupt does not end the wait. <code>harness repos push</code> runs this hook in each repository.</p><h2 id="pre-commit-wiki" tabindex="-1"><code>pre-commit-wiki</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#pre-commit-wiki" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`pre-commit-wiki`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>pre-commit-wiki</code> is the pre-commit hook of the four prose repositories: <code>Knowledge</code>, <code>Environment</code>, <code>Tasks</code> and <code>Library</code>. <code>harness wiki-lint-hook --apply</code> installs it into each as <code>.githooks/pre-commit</code>, and sets <code>core.hooksPath</code>.</p><p>The hook is a Python script that imports only the standard library, in the closed set of Python 3.0 forms of <code>bin/harness</code>, so an old <code>python3</code> prints the version refusal of <code>bin/harness</code> instead of a syntax error. It runs <code>harness wiki-lint --quiet</code> with <code>bin/harness</code> of <code>$RESEARCH_ROOT/Harness</code>, or of <code>~/Research/Harness</code> if <code>RESEARCH_ROOT</code> is not set, over all four trees. An ERROR in any tree blocks the commit, even an ERROR in another repository: the trees link to each other, so an edit here can break a link there. A warning does not block. A missing <code>bin/harness</code> blocks too. The lint takes about a second.</p><p>In a repository whose staged <code>pyproject.toml</code> holds a <code>[tool.ruff]</code> table, the hook also runs <code>ruff format --check</code>, <code>ruff check</code> and <code>basedpyright</code> on the staged Python files and the tools&#39; configuration, which it writes into a temporary directory. The tools come from the <code>bin/</code> of the harness&#39;s Python environment; where the environment lacks one, the hook refuses the commit with one line that names <code>harness install --apply</code>. Every check runs, and the hook refuses the commit when one fails. No Julia repository uses this hook; it uses <code>pre-commit</code>.</p><h2 id="nfc-jl" tabindex="-1"><code>nfc.jl</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#nfc-jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`nfc.jl`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>nfc.jl</code> reports or applies Unicode NFC normalisation. In NFC, a letter such as <code>ẋ</code> is one code point, not a base letter and a combining mark. Julia reads both forms as the same name, but a byte search for one form does not find the other.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --startup-file=no</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> nfc.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                     # check every tracked source file here</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --startup-file=no</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> nfc.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --apply</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">             # rewrite them</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --startup-file=no</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> nfc.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> a.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> b.md</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">           # check just these paths</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --startup-file=no</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> nfc.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --apply</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> src/x.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">    # rewrite just this one</span></span></code></pre></div><p>With no path, it checks every tracked file of the repository in the current directory that is valid UTF-8. The exit status is 0 when each checked file is NFC, and 1 when one is not or when a named file is not valid UTF-8. With <code>--apply</code>, the exit status is 0 after the rewrite.</p><p>Normalisation can change a string literal. Before <code>--apply</code>, check what reads such a literal, for example a doctest. The <code>pre-commit</code> hook has its own copy of the check, in <code>pre-commit.jl</code>, and names this script for the fix. The rule <code>generated-hooks-and-workflows</code> and the agent <code>julia-pr-shepherd</code> tell an agent to run it.</p><h2 id="explicit-imports-jl" tabindex="-1"><code>explicit-imports.jl</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#explicit-imports-jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`explicit-imports.jl`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>explicit-imports.jl</code> runs ExplicitImports.jl on a package and prints what it finds. It loads the package, so it sees the real bindings. <code>fatou lint</code> cannot do this, because its <code>unused-import</code> rule does not follow <code>include</code>.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">pkg</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> explicit-imports.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">PkgNam</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">e</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>It prints the implicit imports, the names that a <code>using</code> brings in without a name list. Then it runs four checks and prints <code>ok</code> or <code>FAIL</code> for each: stale explicit imports, explicit imports through their owner, self-qualified access, and qualified access through the owner. It changes no file. Run it on Julia 1.11 or later, because the ownership checks are reliable only on that version. No hook, skill or agent calls it.</p><h2 id="format-tree-jl" tabindex="-1"><code>format-tree.jl</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#format-tree-jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`format-tree.jl`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>format-tree.jl</code> runs JuliaFormatter over every tracked <code>.jl</code> file of the repositories below <code>Packages/</code> and <code>Experiments/</code>.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> format-tree.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">            # report only, changes nothing</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> format-tree.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --apply</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">    # rewrite files in place</span></span></code></pre></div><p>It formats each repository with its own <code>.JuliaFormatter.toml</code>, and skips a repository with no such file. It skips the directories <code>prototyping/</code>, <code>legacy/</code> and <code>obsolete/</code>. It works on one file at a time, and writes the name of each file to a marker file before it starts.</p><p><code>harness format</code> runs this script. When JuliaFormatter crashes the process on a file, <code>harness format</code> moves that file to a skip list and runs the script again, until it ends cleanly. Use <code>harness format</code>, not the script, for a run over the tree. For the files of one change, run JuliaFormatter on those files only.</p><h2 id="release-notes-jl-and-release-notes-test-jl" tabindex="-1"><code>release-notes.jl</code> and <code>release-notes-test.jl</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#release-notes-jl-and-release-notes-test-jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`release-notes.jl` and `release-notes-test.jl`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>release-notes.jl</code> builds the body of the <code>@JuliaRegistrator</code> comment from the <code>CHANGELOG.md</code> of a package. It refuses a breaking release whose notes have no breaking-changes section, because the registry then blocks the release.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --startup-file=no</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> release-notes.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [package-dir] </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> notes.json</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">gh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> api</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> repos/</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">owne</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">r</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">/</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">rep</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">o</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">/commits/</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">SH</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">A</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">/comments</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --input</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> notes.json</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The version comes from <code>Project.toml</code>. The notes are the <code>CHANGELOG.md</code> section of that version. The JSON goes to stdout. On a failure, the script writes the reason to stderr, nothing to stdout, and exits 1. It fails when the section is missing or empty, or when a breaking release has no heading that contains &quot;breaking&quot;. The first version of a package is never breaking. The skill <code>julia-release</code> runs it.</p><p><code>release-notes-test.jl</code> checks the script on fixtures, among them the two cases that must fail with an empty stdout.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --startup-file=no</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> release-notes-test.jl</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="workflows-ci-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>workflows/CI.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#workflows-ci-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`workflows/CI.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>workflows/CI.yml</code> is the CI workflow. <code>harness workflows</code> copies it to <code>.github/workflows/CI.yml</code> of each repository. It runs on a push to <code>main</code> or <code>master</code>, on a tag, on a pull request and by hand. It reads the package name from <code>Project.toml</code> at run time, so one file fits every repository.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">job</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it runs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>test</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the tests on Julia <code>min</code> and <code>1</code>, on Ubuntu, macOS and Windows; <code>pre</code> and <code>nightly</code> on Ubuntu, as information only</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>downgrade</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the tests with each direct dependency of <code>Project.toml</code> and <code>test/Project.toml</code> at the lower bound of its <code>[compat]</code> entry, the two resolved together, on Julia <code>min</code>; information only</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>doctest</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>doctest</code> of the package on Julia <code>1</code> on Ubuntu, where <code>docs/Project.toml</code> exists</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The matrix names Julia versions by alias, <code>min</code> and <code>1</code>, so the names of the checks stay the same in every repository. Branch protection needs those fixed names. Only the entry of Julia <code>1</code> on Ubuntu measures coverage and sends it to Codecov. A repository that needs another job gets its own extra workflow file, not an edit of this one.</p><h2 id="workflows-documenter-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>workflows/Documenter.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#workflows-documenter-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`workflows/Documenter.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>workflows/Documenter.yml</code> is the documentation workflow. It builds the documentation with <code>docs/make.jl</code> and deploys it. <code>harness workflows</code> copies it to <code>.github/workflows/Documenter.yml</code> of each repository that has a tracked <code>docs/make.jl</code>.</p><p>It does not go into a repository that the profile key <code>docs_exceptions</code> or <code>docs_additions</code> names. Such a repository keeps its own file: its documentation is a pipeline of several jobs, or it needs an extra step. The doctests do not run here; the <code>doctest</code> job of <code>CI.yml</code> runs them. It runs on the same events as <code>CI.yml</code>, on Ubuntu with Julia <code>1</code>.</p><h2 id="workflows-tagbot-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>workflows/TagBot.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#workflows-tagbot-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`workflows/TagBot.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>workflows/TagBot.yml</code> runs the action <code>JuliaRegistries/TagBot</code> for a registered package. <code>harness workflows</code> copies it to <code>.github/workflows/TagBot.yml</code> of each package below <code>Packages/</code>, and not into an experiment.</p><p>It runs on a comment that the registry posts as <code>JuliaTagBot</code>, and by hand, with the input <code>lookback</code>. An <code>if:</code> condition stops it on every other issue comment.</p><h2 id="workflows-codecov-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>workflows/codecov.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#workflows-codecov-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`workflows/codecov.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>workflows/codecov.yml</code> is the Codecov configuration. <code>harness workflows</code> copies it to <code>codecov.yml</code> at the root of each repository.</p><p>It sets a threshold of 1 % on both the project status and the patch status. Without the threshold, Codecov fails a pull request for any decrease of the coverage. With it, a real regression still fails, but a small drift does not. The coverage comes from one entry of the matrix of <code>CI.yml</code>.</p><h2 id="workflows-dependabot-yml" tabindex="-1"><code>workflows/dependabot.yml</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#workflows-dependabot-yml" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`workflows/dependabot.yml`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>workflows/dependabot.yml</code> is the Dependabot configuration. <code>harness workflows</code> copies it to <code>.github/dependabot.yml</code> of each repository.</p><p>Each week, Dependabot proposes to raise the <code>[compat]</code> bounds of the root <code>Project.toml</code>. It does not update <code>test/Project.toml</code>, <code>docs/Project.toml</code> or the GitHub actions. Change the version of an action in the templates, so that the copies stay identical. The configuration ignores the standard libraries, whose bound stays at &quot;1&quot;.</p><h2 id="verify-workflows-jl" tabindex="-1"><code>verify-workflows.jl</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#verify-workflows-jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`verify-workflows.jl`&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>verify-workflows.jl</code> checks that the workflows of every repository below <code>Packages/</code> and <code>Experiments/</code> match the templates.</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> verify-workflows.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">            # report</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> verify-workflows.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --quiet</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">    # only deviations and the summary</span></span></code></pre></div><p>It checks these points:</p><ol><li><code>CI.yml</code>, <code>dependabot.yml</code>, <code>TagBot.yml</code> and <code>codecov.yml</code> are byte-identical to the templates, in the working tree. <code>TagBot.yml</code> must be absent in an experiment.</li><li>The same files on the default branch, for a repository that is on another branch.</li><li>The job names of <code>CI.yml</code> are the names of the required checks.</li><li>No <code>Register.yml</code>, <code>Documentation.yml</code>, <code>CompatHelper.yml</code> or other forbidden file exists.</li><li>Each <code>uses:</code> names the expected major version of its action.</li></ol><p>It also checks that a repository of <code>docs_additions</code> keeps the body of <code>Documenter.yml</code>. It reports the repositories whose <code>doctest</code> job skips itself. It exits with status 1 when a repository deviates. It reads the profile for <code>docs_exceptions</code> and <code>docs_additions</code>.</p><p>Run it after <code>harness workflows --apply</code>, and before a change to CI. <code>harness ci-protection --apply</code> refuses to run when it fails. The rule <code>generated-hooks-and-workflows</code> and the agent <code>ci-triage</code> name it.</p>',61)])])}const u=t(i,[["render",n]]);export{k as __pageData,u as default};
